From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 07:44:52 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/30] swgl: shade pixels on a Direct3D 11 device of feature
 level 9_3

gpu_d3d11.h is the runtime for the pixel shaders the build now makes. With
a device attached (SwglGpuAttach, or SwglGpuAttachNewDevice for testing),
a draw whose program has a 9_3 pixel shader works like this:
- the vertex shaders still run on the CPU, as before;
- each quad's position and varyings are packed (gpu_pack) into a dynamic
  vertex buffer, with flat values that overflow going into a float
  texture;
- the GPU rasterises and shades them into GPU copies of SWGL's textures.

Every texture keeps its CPU buffer. Two flags say which copy is newer:
- before the GPU uses a texture, newer CPU content is uploaded, and a
  pending full delayed clear becomes a GPU clear;
- prepare_texture(), which every CPU reader goes through, reads newer GPU
  content back first;
- TexSubImage2D updates the GPU copy in place when the copies agree.
Depth moves between the two as flattened runs, which are D24S8 texels bit
for bit.

A draw stays on the CPU when its program has no GPU shader, when its blend
mode has no Direct3D form (advanced and SWGL's own), or when a sampled
texture's format has no GPU form. Nothing changes for contexts without a
device.

Two lessons from the first frames:
- SWGL fills the vertex lanes of a quad in polygon order (0 1 3 2), so
  the index pattern is a fan;
- the pass-through vertex shaders write the texture coordinates before
  SV_Position, because Direct3D links the stages by position and the 9_3
  pixel shaders cannot declare SV_Position.

Wrench: SWGL_GPU=hw|warp attaches a device capped at 9_3, and
SWGL_GPU_TRACE=1 traces every draw, clear, upload and readback. On the PC
the text reftest now renders pixel for pixel as the CPU does, with the
composite on the GPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
